---
import GuideArticleLayout from '../../layouts/GuideArticleLayout.astro';
---

<GuideArticleLayout 
	title="Using OnScreenTimer for Study & Focus" 
	description="Practical tips on using the timer, tasks, and presets for focused study sessions."
>
	<section class="space-y-4">
		<h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-[1.65rem] font-semibold text-zinc-950 dark:text-zinc-50 tracking-tight">Focusing with the Timer</h2>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			A visible countdown is a simple but effective way to maintain focus. By setting a specific boundary for your work session, you can encourage yourself to stay on task until the timer reaches zero.
		</p>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			A popular approach is the 25-minute focused session. Simply enter <code class="font-mono text-zinc-800 dark:text-zinc-200">25</code>, press Start, and work continuously until the chime sounds. Afterwards, take a short break before starting another session.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-4">
		<h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-[1.65rem] font-semibold text-zinc-950 dark:text-zinc-50 tracking-tight">Managing Distractions with Full Mode</h2>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			When you need maximum concentration, switch the display size to <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100">Full</strong>. This mode removes the header, footer, and side panels, leaving only the essential timer controls on screen. 
		</p>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			Combined with your browser's native fullscreen feature, this creates a highly distraction-free environment.
		</p>
		<p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 mt-2">
			<a href="/guide/display-modes" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">Learn more about Display Modes &rarr;</a>
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-4">
		<h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-[1.65rem] font-semibold text-zinc-950 dark:text-zinc-50 tracking-tight">Using Current Tasks</h2>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			The Mini To-do allows you to record exactly what you are working on. Promoting a task to <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100">Current</strong> while a Timer or Stopwatch is running acts as an active commitment. 
		</p>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			OnScreenTimer tracks the elapsed time for each active task, giving you a clear record of how much time you spent on a specific assignment or problem when it's marked as Completed.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-4">
		<h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-[1.65rem] font-semibold text-zinc-950 dark:text-zinc-50 tracking-tight">Structuring with Study Block Presets</h2>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			If you find a single long timer daunting, try breaking your study session into structured phases using a <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100">Study Block Preset</strong>.
		</p>
		<p class="text-base sm:text-[16px] text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
			For example, the <em class="text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100">Learn &rarr; Recall &rarr; Solve &rarr; Review</em> sequence divides an hour of study into specific, manageable steps. By moving from reading to active recall and problem-solving, you can maintain engagement and improve retention.
		</p>
		<p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 mt-2">
			<a href="/guide/study-block-presets" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">Explore all available Study Block Presets &rarr;</a>
		</p>
	</section>

</GuideArticleLayout>
